:knowledge-point/questions.:question/problem:
Express $$
\displaystyle \frac{1}{4}-\frac{1}{2}{\sin }^{2}⁡{36}^{∘}
$$ as a single trigonometric ratio.

:knowledge-point/questions.:question/worked-solution:
Write $u=\cos^2\left(41^\circ\right)$. Expanding the square gives

$$
\begin{aligned}2(2u-1)^2-1 &=8u^2-8u+1\end{aligned}
$$

Regroup the given expression:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&8\cos^4\left(41^\circ\right)-8\cos^2\left(41^\circ\right)+1\\&\qquad=2\left(2\cos^2\left(41^\circ\right)-1\right)^2-1
\end{aligned}
$$

Use $2\cos^2 x-1=\cos(2x)$ on the inner expression:

$$
\begin{aligned}
2\cos^2\left(41^\circ\right)-1&=\cos\left(82^\circ\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute, then apply the cosine form once more:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&2\left(2\cos^2\left(41^\circ\right)-1\right)^2-1\\&\qquad=2\cos^2\left(82^\circ\right)-1\\&\qquad=\cos\left(164^\circ\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Write $u=\sin^2\left(\frac{2\pi}{9}\right)$. Expanding the square gives

$$
\begin{aligned}2(1-2u)^2-1 &=8u^2-8u+1\end{aligned}
$$

Regroup the given expression:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&8\sin^4\left(\frac{2\pi}{9}\right)-8\sin^2\left(\frac{2\pi}{9}\right)+1\\&\qquad=2\left(1-2\sin^2\left(\frac{2\pi}{9}\right)\right)^2-1
\end{aligned}
$$

Use $1-2\sin^2 x=\cos(2x)$ on the inner expression:

$$
\begin{aligned}
1-2\sin^2\left(\frac{2\pi}{9}\right)&=\cos\left(\frac{4\pi}{9}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute, then apply the cosine form once more:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&2\left(1-2\sin^2\left(\frac{2\pi}{9}\right)\right)^2-1\\&\qquad=2\cos^2\left(\frac{4\pi}{9}\right)-1\\&\qquad=\cos\left(\frac{8\pi}{9}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Write $u=\sin^2\left(32^\circ\right)$. Expanding the square gives

$$
\begin{aligned}2(1-2u)^2-1 &=8u^2-8u+1\end{aligned}
$$

Regroup the given expression:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&8\sin^4\left(32^\circ\right)-8\sin^2\left(32^\circ\right)+1\\&\qquad=2\left(1-2\sin^2\left(32^\circ\right)\right)^2-1
\end{aligned}
$$

Use $1-2\sin^2 x=\cos(2x)$ on the inner expression:

$$
\begin{aligned}
1-2\sin^2\left(32^\circ\right)&=\cos\left(64^\circ\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute, then apply the cosine form once more:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&2\left(1-2\sin^2\left(32^\circ\right)\right)^2-1\\&\qquad=2\cos^2\left(64^\circ\right)-1\\&\qquad=\cos\left(128^\circ\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Write $u=\cos^2\left(\frac{\pi}{18}\right)$. Expanding the square gives

$$
\begin{aligned}2(2u-1)^2-1 &=8u^2-8u+1\end{aligned}
$$

Regroup the given expression:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&8\cos^4\left(\frac{\pi}{18}\right)-8\cos^2\left(\frac{\pi}{18}\right)+1\\&\qquad=2\left(2\cos^2\left(\frac{\pi}{18}\right)-1\right)^2-1
\end{aligned}
$$

Use $2\cos^2 x-1=\cos(2x)$ on the inner expression:

$$
\begin{aligned}
2\cos^2\left(\frac{\pi}{18}\right)-1&=\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{9}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute, then apply the cosine form once more:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&2\left(2\cos^2\left(\frac{\pi}{18}\right)-1\right)^2-1\\&\qquad=2\cos^2\left(\frac{\pi}{9}\right)-1\\&\qquad=\cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{9}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
